  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 AM EDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nFairly quiet weather continues the rest of the work week as high \npressure ridging flattens and heads east towards New England today. \nWith flow aloft becoming more zonal (and the U.P. becoming placed \nbehind the center of the high), expect warm air advection to move \ninto the region, allowing temperatures to warm well above normal the \nrest of this week into this weekend. Thus, expect highs to range \nfrom the mid 70s to lower 80s today to the 80s to lower 90s by this \nweekend. In addition, the pleasant and drier conditions we've \nexperienced yesterday are projected to give way to more humid sfc \nconditions by this weekend. Therefore, today and Friday may be the \nbetter days to enjoy the outdoors and get any yardwork done when \ncompared to this upcoming weekend. \n\nOur next real shot of precipitation looks to come around Saturday \nwhen a cold front descends from the Arrowhead/northwestern Ontario \ninto the region. Currently, the severe weather threat doesn't look \ntoo impressive as the western U.P. should be seeing the showers and \nthunderstorms along the front during the morning hours when diurnal \nCAPE is lowest; by the time the front gets into the central and \neastern U.P. by the afternoon hours, any convection that forms will \nhave lost LLJ support (not to mention that bulk shear throughout the \nprofile will decrease with time). Nevertheless, cannot completely \nrule out the severe weather threat across the area as the frontal \nboundary does look fairly robust, particularly during the morning \nhours; this boundary could easily mechanically force parcels through \nthe boundary layer cap into the free atmosphere. While the medium \nrange model soundings are showing fairly tall, skinny CAPE aloft, \nthere does look to be a couple thousand J/kg available to get \nconvection going near and along the frontal boundary. Therefore, \nthere remains a non-zero chance for severe hail and winds with the \ncold front's passage on Saturday. \n\nRain chances return Sunday and Monday via an incoming Clipper \nshortwave early next week. With 850mb temperatures increasing to \nabove 20C ahead of the Clipper's arrival, the atmosphere is expected \nto be juiced and primed for convection. Whether we see much \n(including severe weather) will depend on the track of the Clipper \nshortwave and its preceding warm front; should the Clipper track \nnorth or south of our area, we may avoid much of the convection and \nsee no severe weather (same can be said for the warm front moving in \non Sunday); will continue to monitor the track of this shortwave \ngiven the potential impacts that it could have over our area. \n\nMoving into the rest of next week, expect high pressure ridging to \nbuild over the central CONUS behind the Clipper low. While this \ncould set us up for some cooler/near-normal temperatures and dry \nweather for next Tuesday, depending on how strong the high pressure \nis, we could see more Clipper shortwaves move across the area or \nhigh pressure ridging keep us dry for the remainder of the week. \nRegardless, expect temperatures to start climbing to above normal \nonce again by the middle of next week.",
